Our ability to declare and pay regular or special dividends on our common stock and repurchase shares of our common stock is subject to certain considerations.

Regular and special dividends on our common stock and repurchases of shares of our common stock are authorized and determined by our Board in its sole discretion and depend upon a number of factors, including:

• cash available for dividends;

• cash available for share repurchases;

• our results of operations and anticipated future results of operations;

16

• our financial condition, especially in relation to the anticipated capital requirements necessary to conduct our operations and carry out our business strategy;

• our operating costs;

• any contractual restrictions or statutory/legal restrictions;

• the levels of dividends paid by comparable companies; and

• other factors our Board deems relevant.

We expect to continue to pay dividends to our stockholders; however, any determination by our Board to declare and pay dividends in the future is solely within the discretion of our Board. Accordingly, our Board may reduce our dividends or cease declaring dividends at any time, including if it determines that our current or forecasted future cash flows provided by our operations (and after consideration of our other capital expenditure and other commitments requiring cash) are not sufficient to pay our desired levels of dividends to our stockholders or to pay dividends to our stockholders at all. Any reduction in the amount of dividends we pay to stockholders could have an adverse effect on the trading price of our common stock.

In November 2021, our Board established a share repurchase authorization (the “Share Repurchase Authorization”) authorizing the repurchase by us of up to $10 billion of our common stock. We have repurchased shares from time to time under the Share Repurchase Authorization. The timing and amount of any share repurchases is at the discretion of our management and depends upon, among other factors, general business and market conditions, corporate and regulatory requirements, the trading price of our common stock and capital availability. The Share Repurchase Authorization may be suspended or discontinued at any time and does not obligate us to repurchase any specific number of shares of common stock.

Our hedging activities may prevent us from fully benefiting from increases in commodity prices and may expose us to other risks, including counterparty risk, and our future production levels and cash flows may not be protected from declines in commodity prices by our existing or future hedging arrangements.

We use financial derivative instruments (primarily financial basis swaps and options contracts) to hedge the impact of fluctuations in crude oil, NGLs and natural gas prices on our results of operations and cash flows. To the extent we enter into hedging arrangements to protect against commodity price declines, we may not benefit from increases in commodity prices above the prices established by such arrangements. In addition, our hedging activities may expose us to other risks, including counterparty risk. If the counterparties to our hedging contracts fail to perform under the contracts, our financial condition and results of operations could be adversely affected.

Exploration and Production Regulation

Our operations are subject to various federal, state, tribal and local laws and regulations relating to exploration and production activities, including with respect to:

acquisition of seismic data;

design, location, drilling and casing of wells;

hydraulic fracturing;

well production, and the gathering and transportation of such production;

spill prevention plans;

emissions and discharge permitting;

use, transportation, storage and disposal of fluids and materials incidental to oil and gas operations;

surface usage and the restoration of properties upon which wells have been drilled;

calculation and disbursement of royalty payments and production taxes;

plugging and abandoning of wells; and

endangered species and habitat.

Our operations are also subject to conservation regulations, including the regulation of the size of drilling and spacing units or proration units, the number of wells or the locations at which we can drill. In addition, state conservation laws establish maximum rates of production from oil and natural gas wells and generally limit the venting or flaring of natural gas produced in association with oil production. These laws and regulations may limit the amount of oil and natural gas that we can produce from our wells and may limit the number of wells or the locations at which we can drill, although they generally do not limit the total hydrocarbon reserves that can be produced from a given underlying reservoir.

Certain of our leases are granted or approved by the federal government and administered by the Department of the Interior and the Department of Agriculture. These leases contain additional regulatory, environmental and reporting requirements that are administered by the Bureau of Land Management and the U.S. Forest Service, respectively. In addition, many states that contain federal onshore lands have entered into cooperative agreements with these federal agencies to allow the state to receive a portion of the royalty revenues generated from federal leases and to regulate these federal leases under state standards.

Operations on federal oil and gas leases must comply with numerous statutes and regulations. For example, the Mineral Leasing Act of 1920 and the Federal Land Policy and Management Act require the payment of annual rentals and minimum royalties on producing leaseholds, reclamation standards for onshore federal lands and the posting of bonds under certain circumstances. Additionally, any exploration and production activities conducted on federal lands must comply with other federal laws and regulations, including the National Environmental Policy Act, the Endangered Species Act, the Clean Water Act and the Clean Air Act. Each of these statutes requires compliance with a broad range of environmental and public health and safety requirements, including potential permitting or other governmental approvals to conduct exploration, development and production operations. Failure to comply with these requirements could result in civil or criminal penalties, the imposition of remedial obligations, the restriction or suspension of operations and/or the cancellation or forfeiture of leases.

State and local governments also have the authority to regulate oil and natural gas exploration and production activities in their jurisdictions. Permit and approval requirements for such activities vary by state and locality.

The regulatory burden on the exploration and production industry increases the cost of doing business and affects profitability. Although we make every effort to comply with applicable laws and regulations, action taken by the regulatory agencies may result in increases in operational and compliance costs, delays and potential civil or criminal penalties.

These laws and regulations and any changes thereto could significantly affect our exploration and production activities or our customers’ activities or otherwise adversely impact operations. The federal government has recently pursued more vigorous enforcement of applicable environmental statutes and regulations, and this trend is expected to continue in the near future.

 ITEM 1C. CYBERSECURITY

 Governance
Our Board of Directors, with assistance from our Audit Committee and Sustainability Committee, oversees our cybersecurity risks. The Board receives management reports on significant cybersecurity matters at every regularly scheduled Board meeting, and the Vice President of Information Technology (VP-IT) or his or her designee reports to the Board on material cybersecurity matters outside of regularly scheduled Board meetings.

The Audit Committee is charged with oversight of management’s risk assessments and risk management activities (including cybersecurity). The Audit Committee reviews with our Chief Financial Officer (CFO), our VP-IT and our Chief Compliance Officer, and auditors, as appropriate, the Company’s internal controls, including those related to cybersecurity. The Sustainability Committee is charged with oversight of the Company’s ESG programs and practices, including cybersecurity. The Sustainability Committee reviews with our management team the Company’s policies, initiatives and milestones related to ESG matters, including cybersecurity. The Board and its committees periodically receive updates from our management team, including our VP-IT, on cybersecurity.

Our management team has the primary responsibility for assessing and managing cybersecurity risks. The VP-IT reports to our CFO and leads our cybersecurity program and strategy—including incident prevention, detection and response measures. The VP-IT works with other members of management on cybersecurity risk matters. Our VP-IT has over 20 years of experience in IT and cybersecurity roles across a variety of industries. Our VP-IT also holds multiple industry certifications, including the Certified Information Systems Security Professional (CISSP) and the Certified Information Security Manager (CISM). Our IT team also includes experienced personnel that hold other relevant industry certifications, including the Certified Information Systems Auditor (CISA) and the Certified Ethical Hacker (CEH) certifications. Our VP-IT and the IT team actively monitor and analyze cybersecurity threats and the effectiveness of our cybersecurity controls. We maintain relationships with external cybersecurity experts to assist our IT team in developing the Company’s strategy related to cybersecurity.

We maintain policies and procedures on cybersecurity, including policies requiring employees and contractors to comply with applicable cybersecurity requirements and report suspected incidents. We also maintain cybersecurity incident response and disaster recovery plans.

Our cybersecurity program includes controls designed to identify, prevent and mitigate risks and to detect anomalies and respond to cybersecurity incidents. Our controls and processes are intended to identify, assess and manage material risks from cybersecurity threats. Our controls and processes include:

• risk assessments;

• policies and procedures; 

• access controls, including multi-factor authentication;

• network protection tools; 

• monitoring tools; 

• vulnerability management tools;

• incident response and disaster recovery planning; 

• vendor management processes; and

• cybersecurity training and awareness programs.

We maintain third-party risk management processes to oversee, identify and reduce cybersecurity risks associated with our use of third-party service providers, including vendors and suppliers. Our third-party risk management program also includes cybersecurity requirements and due diligence procedures, continuous monitoring and third-party incident response processes.

We perform periodic assessments of our cybersecurity program, including internal assessments by our IT team and external assessments by third parties. We conduct periodic training for our employees and contractors on cybersecurity, including phishing simulations and other exercises.

During the year ended December 31, 2023, we did not experience any cybersecurity incidents that materially affected our business strategy, results of operations or financial condition. However, we face ongoing cybersecurity threats from outside parties and from insiders. For additional risks related to cybersecurity, see “Risk Factors—Risks Related to Our Business—Our business is subject to the risk of cyber-attacks and other similar disruptions.” We maintain cybersecurity insurance policies; however, insurance may not cover all costs, expenses or losses arising from a cybersecurity incident. In addition, our cybersecurity insurance policies include significant deductibles and may include other limitations and exclusions.

 Risk Management and Strategy
We maintain a cybersecurity Incident Response Plan (“IRP”) designed to identify, assess, manage, mitigate, and remediate cybersecurity incidents, and to recover and restore our business operations. Our IRP outlines the responsibilities of the IT team and the Company’s management, including the roles and responsibilities of the Cybersecurity Incident Response Team (CIRT), procedures for responding to cybersecurity incidents, reporting protocols, and guidance for coordination and communication with our Board of Directors, law enforcement and regulatory authorities, public communications, and customers and suppliers. The IRP also addresses third-party cybersecurity incidents, such as incidents experienced by our vendors or suppliers that could affect the Company.

Our cybersecurity program includes training and awareness initiatives that are intended to educate employees and contractors on cybersecurity risks and the Company’s policies and procedures. We conduct periodic phishing simulations and tabletop exercises to test our preparedness and response. Our cybersecurity controls and processes are aligned with commonly used and recognized cybersecurity frameworks, including NIST Cybersecurity Frame

