Risks Related to Our Industry

Volatile Oil, Gas and NGL Prices Significantly Impact Our Business

Our financial condition, results of operations and the value of our business are highly dependent upon the prevailing prices for oil, gas and NGLs. Historically, the markets for oil, gas and NGLs have been volatile and they are likely to continue to be volatile. Prices for oil, gas and NGLs are subject to wide fluctuations in response to relatively minor changes in the global supply of and demand for such commodities and a variety of additional factors beyond our control. These factors include, but are not limited to:

the domestic and worldwide supply of and demand for oil, gas and NGLs;

volatility and trading patterns in the commodity-futures markets;

climate change incentives and conservation and environmental protection efforts;

production levels of members of OPEC, Russia, the U.S. or other producing countries;

geopolitical risks, including the conflict between Russia and Ukraine, and potential destabilization and civil unrest in the Middle East, Africa, Europe and South America;

adverse weather conditions, natural disasters and other catastrophic events (such as tornadoes, earthquakes, hurricanes and acts of terrorism);

pandemics (such as COVID-19) and epidemics of infectious diseases and other public health events, as well as any related governmental actions;

regional pricing differentials, including in the Delaware Basin and other areas of our operations;

differing quality of production, including NGL content of gas produced;

the level of imports and exports of oil, gas and NGLs and the level of global oil, gas and NGL inventories;

the price and availability of alternative energy sources;

technological advances affecting energy consumption and production, including with respect to electric vehicles;

stockholder activism or activities by non-governmental organizations or legislatures targeting the production of oil and natural gas in order to reduce GHG emissions;

the discovery rates of new oil, gas and NGL reserves and the availability and cost of capital for drilling, development and operating activities;

changes in economic conditions in the U.S. and globally, including inflation, interest rate fluctuations, success or failure of calmatives in the U.S. and elsewhere, labor shortages and supply chain disruptions;

U.S. oil, gas and NGL inventories;

U.S. exports of oil, gas and NGLs;

foreign and domestic governmental regulations and taxes;

political conditions or hostilities in oil and gas producing areas;

nuclear disarmament and nonproliferation;

terrorist attacks and concerns;

significant governmental and environmental regulation;

infrastructure constraints, including transportation, processing and storage capacity;

changes in the global economy and financial markets; and

the effect of worldwide energy conservation measures and related governmental policies.

Any one or more of the foregoing factors, or other factors, could adversely affect the prices of oil, gas and NGLs and, in turn, could adversely affect our business, results of operations and financial condition and could result in, among other things: (i) a reduction in our cash flows; (ii) reductions in the carrying value of our properties; (iii) investors not willing to invest in the fossil energy sector; (iv) negative impacts to our borrowing base and/or our ability to borrow additional funds under our senior credit facility; (v) reductions to the volumes of oil, gas and NGL reserves we can economically produce and the associated revenues; and (vi) delays in our full capital development projects and/or the reallocation of such capital to other projects.

Further, sudden and significant price declines could adversely affect the threat of improving oil and gas production, global supply and demand dynamics and the U.S. and global economies; the susceptibility of commodity markets to such declines has been acerbated by recent actions of OPEC and its members, including OPEC+, and Russia; and (ii) economic sanctions, including embargoes, on Russia or on other oil-producing countries by the U.S.; and

other governmental regulations and taxes.

