Our Operations Are Uncertain and Involve Substantial Costs and Risks

Our operating activities are subject to numerous costs and risks, many of which are outside our control. These risks include, among other things, the risk that we will not encounter commercially productive oil or gas reservoirs, reservoir performance may be lower than expected, meeting production targets may be delayed or fail to meet our projections for a variety of reasons (including permitting, facility and equipment downtime, operator error and natural events), and well costs may be higher or lower than estimated.

Our exploration and development activities involve numerous risks associated with the inherent uncertainty of oil and gas exploration and development, including the following:

• significant investment costs to identify, evaluate, drill and complete wells and establish or enhance production;

• the risk that the wells we drill will be unproductive or have a shorter productive life than we anticipated;

• the risk that the operational performance of our wells may be lower than expected due to factors such as reservoir characteristics, mechanical or operational issues, or other factors beyond our control;

• the risk of loss of production, loss of reserves, or loss of information and data due to natural disasters, mechanical failures, cyber incidents, vandalism, terrorism, or other unforeseen events;

• risks associated with our dependence on third-party service providers, including the availability, reliability and performance of their products and services, and the potential for disruptions in their supply chains or operations;

• exposure to litigation and regulatory actions related to our operations, including environmental, safety and other compliance matters;

• exposure to cost inflation, supply chain disruptions, and other market conditions that may increase our operating and capital costs; and

• other risks and uncertainties relating to our business and industry.

We face competitive pressures that may adversely affect our ability to acquire, develop and operate oil and gas properties. Our ability to acquire additional reserves and replace our production depends on a variety of factors, including our access to capital, the availability of attractive opportunities and the competitive environment in the oil and gas industry. We compete with other companies for the acquisition of properties, leases and other interests, and for equipment, services and supplies. Some of our competitors have substantially greater financial, technical and other resources than we do and may be better positioned to compete for opportunities and resources. As a result, we may be at a competitive disadvantage in acquiring and developing properties and in operating our assets.

We also face risks associated with our dependence on third-party infrastructure, including pipelines, processing facilities and transportation networks. Disruptions in these systems, whether due to operational issues, weather events, cyber incidents or other causes, could limit our ability to deliver production to market, increase costs or otherwise adversely affect our business and results of operations.

In addition, our business is subject to various risks associated with the regulatory environment in which we operate. Changes in laws, regulations or governmental policies could impose new or more stringent requirements on our operations, increase our costs or otherwise adversely affect our business. We regularly monitor regulatory developments and assess their potential impact on our business and operations. However, we cannot predict with certainty how changes in laws, regulations or policies may affect our business or industry.

We rely on assumptions about market conditions, commodity prices, production volumes and costs to plan our operations and allocate capital. If actual conditions differ materially from our assumptions, our results of operations and financial condition could be adversely affected. We may also use hedging strategies to mitigate some of the risks associated with commodity price volatility and interest rate fluctuations; however, these strategies may not be effective and could expose us to the risk of financial loss in certain circumstances, including counterparty credit risk, basis risk and the potential for hedging arrangements to limit the benefits of commodity price increases above the prices established in our hedging contracts.

 ITEM 1A. RISK FACTORS

 Business and Operational Risks
You should carefully consider the following risk factors in addition to the other information contained in this report in connection with an investment in our common stock, debt securities, or preferred stock.

Commodity prices fluctuate widely, and low prices for an extended period would likely have a material adverse impact on our business.

Our revenues, operating results, financial condition and ability to service our debt are highly dependent on the prices that we receive for oil, natural gas and NGL production resales. Historically, the markets for these products have been volatile and are likely to continue to be volatile in the future. Volatility in these markets results from many factors that are beyond our control, including:

• changes in global supply and demand for oil, natural gas and NGLs;

• political and economic conditions and events in other countries, particularly the Middle East, Russia, South America and other oil and gas producing regions;

• U.S. federal, state and local and non-U.S. governmental regulations and taxes;

• capacity constraints of transportation and storage facilities;

• the availability of goods and services; and

• the impact of war and terrorism.

The average NYMEX oil and natural gas prices and regional oil price differentials have fluctuated widely over the past few years and are likely to continue to fluctuate significantly in the future. If the prices of oil, natural gas and NGLs decline and remain low for an extended period of time, we may, among other things:

• suspend or delay some of our drilling and other development activities;

• reduce our planned capital expenditures;

• defer planned projects and completions;

• curtail development and production of reserves due to a lack of adequate cash flows;

• sell some of our assets or seek joint ventures or partnerships;

• reduce cash dividends or share repurchases; and

• reduce our workforce or implement other cost-saving measures.

All of these factors and the conditions we face will materially and adversely impact our revenues, operating results and financial condition. Any future decrease in commodity prices could adversely affect our results of operations, our ability to execute our capital program and our ability to realize the value of our assets.

We could also experience long-term negative impacts to our business as a result of sustained low commodity prices, including a reduction in the value of our proved reserves. Sustained low commodity prices may result in impairments of our oil and gas properties or long-lived assets. A sustained lower commodity price environment may also negatively impact our ability to access capital markets and finance our operations on attractive terms or at all.

