Exploration and Production Regulation

Our operations are subject to various federal, state, tribal and local laws and regulations that govern the exploration and production of oil and gas, including environmental matters associated with oil and gas operations. In addition to compliance with these laws and regulations, our operations are also affected by court decisions and the decisions and orders of federal and state agencies. Laws and regulations affecting exploration and production activities include, among others, the following:

acquisition of seismic data;

design, location, drilling and casing of wells;

hydraulic fracturing and disclosing the composition of fracturing fluids;

well production operations;

formation stimulation and well workovers;

water withdrawal;

use, transportation and disposal of nonhazardous and hazardous substances and wastes;

use of underground injection wells, including in connection with enhanced oil recovery and disposal of fluids;

gathering and transportation of such production;

spill prevention and spill response plans;

venting and flaring;

surface usage and the restoration of properties involved in exploration and production;

development in or near environmentally sensitive areas and habitats of endangered species;

financial assurance and bonding requirements;

payment of royalties, bonuses, fines, penalties and other payments related to our properties and operations; and

abandonment, reclamation and remediation of well and other sites.

Other Environmental Laws and Regulations. In addition to the regulations addressed above, we may be subject to a variety of other local, state and federal laws and regulations, including those pertaining to endangered species, cultural resources and the management of hazardous and nonhazardous waste. New regulations and requirements may be implemented by federal or state agencies from time to time. For example, the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) has expanded its regulations and oversight related to air emissions, including emissions from oil and gas production facilities, and certain states have enacted laws and regulations relating to air emissions from oil and gas sources that are more stringent than federal standards and/or apply to categories of sources (e.g., storage vessels) that are not regulated at the federal level. In addition, some states have adopted regulations aimed at reducing methane emissions from oil and gas operations, which have included mandates for new leak detection and retrofitting requirements for existing equipment. These laws and regulations, as well as any new or amended laws and regulations, could result in increased compliance costs, capital expenditures and operational restrictions. We cannot predict the impact of any subsequent laws, regulations or requirements that may be adopted, nor can we predict whether any future laws, regulations or requirements will have a material effect on our results of operations or financial condition.

